      "score": 44,
      "reasoning": "This is Objective (Mode A). The report accurately reproduces the cumulative loss, both half-period rates, 36% acceleration, and 2023 maximum, with detailed and largely sound methodology; however, it does not quantitatively assess or substantiate the key comparison that glacier loss exceeded Greenland by 18% and Antarctic loss by more than twofold, and it disputes rather than reproduces the published ±10% acceleration uncertainty."
     },
     {
      "index": 1,
      "type": "image",
      "weight": 0.1,
      "score": 36,
      "reasoning": "Mode A. The contribution chart correctly identifies Alaska, combined Canadian Arctic, Greenland periphery, and Southern Andes with mass-loss rates consistent with the target and implicitly reproducing the stated 22%, 20%, 13%, and 10% shares. However, the images never calculate or display loss relative to year-2000 ice volume, so the key −39%, −35%, −29%, −23%, and −20% regional results are missing; specific-rate plots are not an equivalent metric."
     },
     {
      "index": 2,
      "type": "image",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "score": 35,
      "reasoning": "This is Mode A because the criterion specifies quantitative inter-method biases and uncertainties. The agent’s method-comparison figure captures the expected positive altimetry bias and small average gravimetry bias, including conspicuous Alaska and Southern Andes discrepancies, but it does not report the required aggregate values and entirely omits the glaciological mean bias and −0.61 to +0.53 range shown in the target analysis."
     },
     {
      "index": 3,
      "type": "image",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "score": 0,
      "reasoning": "This is an objective visual/quantitative criterion. None of the agent’s figures compares GlaMBIE observations with GlacierMIP2/CMIP6 projections, future emission scenarios, sea-level equivalent, or 2040/2100 projected losses; the report explicitly states that model projections were not assessed, so the target figure’s central content is absent."
     }
